          "id": "WI-39261",
          "kind": "feature",
          "title": "Add unit, integration, and protocol tests for zero stock/“999,” stale price, invented policy, forged/irrelevant citations, tone mismatch, injection, concurrent inventory change, and valid edits. Exer…",
          "state": "done",
          "completedAt": "2026-08-17T19:31:57.794Z",
          "completionAuthority": "committed",
          "completionSummary": "Closed the 3 genuine gaps in the P-005 adversarial matrix; the other 5 scenarios were already built and I re-verified that at HEAD rather than trusting the inherited analysis.\n\nBUILT THIS WAKE (commit 6d44712, +225 lines across 2 files):\n\n1. ZERO STOCK / \"999\" — two cases, both landing on evidence-missing NOT evidence-stale, which is the point: no fact moved, so telling a seller \"the draft is out of date\" sends them to look at a clock that is not the problem. `availability-inflated-beyond-what-the-listing-ever-said` (listing says 12, reply promises 999) and `sold-out-item-called-available-at-binding` (qty was ALREADY 0 when the reply was written — there is no instant at which that reply was true, which separates it from the pre-existing sold-out-AFTER-binding case). Both bound through bind() against the real context, never a hand-written fingerprint.\n\n2. CONCURRENT INVENTORY CHANGE — required extending the case shape. Every fixture carried exactly two contexts (bound, atSend), and with two you cannot tell \"already wrong when the seller approved\" from \"went wrong while the judge was running\" — different fixes. Added `approveWindow: { atApprove, expected }`, grouped so a context can never be supplied without an expectation to assert. Case `stock-runs-out-inside-the-approve-to-send-window`: bound=4, atApprove=4 (the approve-time check has nothing to catch), atSend=0. The PAIR is the assertion — either half alone is satisfiable by a broken guard (\"fails at send\" is also true of a guard that fails everything).\n\n3. VALID EDITS — `seller-edit-keeps-every-claim-grounded`, revision 2 with two claims that both hold. Without it, \"edited replies are judged on their own claims\" was only ever shown by an edit that FAILS, which is equally consistent with a path that holds every edit on sight.\n\nALREADY COVERED — re-verified at HEAD, not rebuilt: stale price ('price-moved-after-binding'), invented policy ('returns-window-misstated'), forged citation ('price-cites-a-source-not-in-the-context'), irrelevant citation ('warranty-claim-cites-the-price-source', 'shipping-claim-cites-a-transcript'), tone (guardrail.test.ts), injection (rehearsals/injection-rehearsal.test.ts + injection-rehearsal.ts). The last two are the ones the inherited analysis was truncated before reaching, so I grepped for them independently rather than assume.\n\nACCEPTANCE — 'the original exploit cannot score/pass or reach chat': held at the contract level. The 999 reply satisfies EVERY structural check a citation-shaped guard can make (source exists, was gathered, covers the question, has not moved) and is still held; the test asserts the naive citationsOnlyVerdict waves it through while verifyClaims does not. That matched pair is the evidence, not the green tick.",
