        {
          "id": "D-007",
          "title": "A stale liveness claim is RE-TENSED, never dropped — and the flag rides on the value that makes the claim",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-19\nSettles the open product question WI-39868 deliberately left un-coded, and generalises it so other surfaces stop re-deciding it.\n\n#### The question\nThe buyer overlay rendered \"On stage: &lt;product&gt;\" whenever the event had EVER staged a product, because the buyer feed is a poll of PERSISTED moments. WI-39868 asked: when the video is not live, drop the line, or re-word it to the past tense?\n\n#### Ruling\n\n**1. RE-TENSE, do not drop.** The overlay now renders \"Last shown: X\" when the video is not live and \"On stage: X\" when it is. Dropping would trade a false claim for a lost one: a buyer arriving mid-replay still wants to know what was featured, and that information is genuinely available — only the tense was wrong. Withholding true information is not the cheap option it looks like; it is a second defect with no error message.\n\n**2. The styling must give up the claim with the words.** `.is-past` drops the emphasis. This is the WI-39839 lesson restated: that fix had to move the pill's colour as well as its label, because `state-listening` green went on asserting what the words had just retracted. A surface makes its claim in every channel it has.\n\n**3. THE MECHANISM — the liveness flag belongs ON the value that makes the claim, and is never optional.** `TranscriptOverlayActiveProduct.live: boolean` (not a sibling field on the presentation, not defaulted).\n\n  - **Not optional**, because the original bug was a surface asserting the STRONG claim by default. `live?: boolean` lets the next call site re-acquire \"On stage\" by not thinking about it — which is exactly how this arrived. Same reasoning as WI-39839's `videoLive`, which is required for the same reason.\n  - **On the product, not beside it**, so the obligation lands precisely where the claim is made. A required sibling field on `TranscriptOverlayPresentation` would be demanded of every caller including the many that never name a product — stranding fixtures with no opinion to record. That pressure is what turns a required field optional again six weeks later, which is how the guard would have been lost.\n\n**4. A present-tense claim is legitimate when it reports the actor's OWN action.** The seller path keeps saying \"On stage\" and sets `live: true` explicitly: their `activeProduct` is the product THEY staged, read back — not an inference from what the captions happened to mention. It stays true between utterances. The buyer path cannot make that claim because it has no such source, which is the whole distinction.\n\n#### Scope for other lanes\nAny surface deriving a present-tense statement from persisted/polled data owes the same treatment: name the subject you can actually observe, carry the liveness discriminator on the value rather than beside it, make it required, and re-tense rather than withhold. See fact `sidestage-liveness-labels-must-name-their-own-subject`.\n\n#### Evidence\n`apps/web/src/{LiveTranscriptOverlay,VideoEngagementOverlay}.tsx` + `live-transcript-overlay.css`; 3 new tests asserting at the RENDERED-MARKUP level (a presentation-object test cannot see whether anything renders it — WI-39839's first symptom shipped as a correct-but-uncalled helper for exactly that reason), including a same-fixture/opposite-input control. sidestage typecheck 0; web suite 109 files / 958 tests green; falsifiability proven by 2 in-tree mutation probes (render fork pinned to \"On stage:\", buyer presentation hardcoded to `live: true`) — both CAUGHT, tree byte-identical after each.",
          "date": "2026-08-19",
          "itemRefs": [],
          "lineNumber": 114
        }
