      "decisions": [
        {
          "id": "D-001",
          "title": "The judge panel keeps its bespoke markup — the shared RehearsalPanel does NOT replace it",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-14\nP-008 as written asked the shared RehearsalPanel to replace \"the bespoke judge markup shape\". The panel was built and is used by all four rehearsals, but the judge panel was deliberately LEFT AS IS.\n\nWhy: the judge report is not a RehearsalReport. Every judge case carries PER-DIMENSION scores (a score + a justification per dimension, plus a weighted overall), and the generic panel models a case as a single pass/fail with an expectation and an observation. Converting the judge panel to the generic shape would have to drop the per-dimension breakdown or smuggle it through a field that does not mean that — i.e. the conversion LOSES information that the judge view exists to show.\n\nThe part of P-008 that carries the actual value — one uniform shape for every report that IS a RehearsalReport, showing expectation beside observed — is delivered. Convergence for its own sake is not.\n\nRULING FOR OTHER LANES: do not \"finish\" this by converting the judge panel. If a future change genuinely needs one component for both, the correct move is to widen RehearsalCaseResult to model scored dimensions first, and that is a contract change to the API-side rehearsal types, not a web-only refactor.\nRelated: P-008",
          "date": "2026-08-14",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-008"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 40
        },
        {
          "id": "D-002",
          "title": "P-007 ships the scoping half; the three measured probes are deferred, not silently dropped",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-14\nP-007 bundled four things: (a) a realtime round-trip probe (measured), (b) a media loopback / device check, (c) a clock-skew check, and (d) replacing the hardcoded event name with the live one.\n\nSHIPPED: (d), plus the server-side setup check (P-006) which covers the honest half of what (a)-(c) were reaching for — DB reachability, migration state, search-index parity, guardrail config — from the side that can actually measure it.\n\nDEFERRED: (a), (b), (c). Reason: each needs real instrumentation to be truthful, and a probe that reports \"ok\" because it did not actually measure anything is worse than no probe on a screen whose entire purpose is to tell a host whether they are safe to go live. A green checkmark that is not backed by a measurement is the specific failure this feature exists to prevent, so shipping the placeholder version would have contradicted the plan's own goal.\n\nTracked as a follow-up work-item rather than left implicit. Do not mark this plan's intent unmet on account of the deferral, and do not re-add the probes as unmeasured placeholders.\nRelated: P-007",
          "date": "2026-08-14",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-007",
            "P-006"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 51
        },
        {
          "id": "D-003",
          "title": "Correcting D-002 — P-006 shipped neither a migration-state nor an index-parity check, and its DB check reported a boot-time fact in the present tense",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-14\nD-002 records P-006 as having shipped \"DB reachability, migration state, search-index parity, guardrail config\". Acceptance scorecard EI-20394181989843574 graded `measured-not-asserted` DEGRADED against that sentence. On verification the grade is right, so the record is corrected here rather than by editing D-002 — the original claim is cited in WI-38672's completion record and must stay legible.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG\n- \"migration state\": SideStage has NO migration system. Zero migration files and zero .sql files under apps/api. There was never anything to check.\n- \"search-index parity\": no parity check exists. Typesense is used by the catalog (catalog.sources.ts) behind a deliberate SQL fallback, so a missing index DEGRADES search rather than blocking a launch. There is no parity invariant to measure, which is why none was written.\n- \"DB reachability\": a check did exist, but it was `this.pool !== null`. One nuance the grade did not carry: createPoolOrNull DOES run `SELECT 1` at startup, so a non-null pool means Postgres answered ONCE, at boot. The defect was therefore not \"never measured\" but \"measured at boot, reported in the present tense\" — which, on a screen a host opens in the minutes before going live, is the same lie with a better alibi. preflight.ts's own header forbids exactly this: \"A check that could not be measured must never render as a green light.\"\n\nWHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION\nThe guardrail-config half of D-002 is accurate and unchanged — the config and policy lints are real and branch-derived, and were graded healthy. D-002's DEFERRAL reasoning for P-007's three client probes also stands: they remain deferred, tracked as WI-38675, and the grade confirmed they are absent rather than faked, which was the point of deferring them.\n\nWHAT CHANGED (WI-38681)\nDurability is now a LIVE probe on every preflight request, modelled as four cases: reachable (with measured latency) / unreachable / unknown / absent. `unknown` — a status this module declared \"first-class on purpose\" and then never once returned — is emitted when the probe times out, and PreflightReport.ready now requires `blockers === 0 && unknowns === 0`, so an unmeasured check can no longer render green. A wired-but-dead database is a BLOCKER, not a warning: the stores bound themselves to Postgres at boot, so every write during the event would fail — strictly worse than the no-database case, which falls back to memory and still works. Both new guards were mutation-proved in an isolated git worktree (shared tree untouched): dropping the unknowns clause and asserting reachability without issuing a query each turn the suite red.\n\nRULING FOR OTHER LANES\nDo not \"finish\" this by building a migration-state or index-parity check. Neither subsystem exists, and inventing one so that a decision sentence becomes true is backwards. If SideStage later gains migrations, a migration-state check becomes a real item at that point — not before.\n\nRelated: P-006, P-007, WI-38681, EI-20394181989843574",
          "date": "2026-08-14",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-006",
            "P-007"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 62
        }
      ],
